<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Average argument ranking performance of LLMs across the three acts and overall on 12AngryMen, using different instruction strategies. We report Spearman’s <math id="S5.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and Kendall’s <math id="S5.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, bolding the best-performing technique per act or overall. As in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5 Results ‣ Can LLMs Judge Debates?Evaluating Non-Linear Reasoning via Argumentation Theory Semantics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, all methods improve on the baseline; CoT few-shot is top overall. Stronger results on Act 3, similar in size to DebatePedia, suggest debate length affects performance.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
